`t/90-live-idrac.t:45-55` restores the power state the server was found in when the test dies half way. For a server that was **off** at the start it does so with `$idrac->power->off( force => 1 )` (line 50), i.e. `ForceOff`, unconditionally.

The header of the same file (lines 16-18) and `CLAUDE.md` promise the opposite: the server is switched off by asking its operating system, and the power is only cut when `IDRAC_LIVE_FORCE_OFF=1` allows it. The switching block honours that (lines 129-135: `$gentle ? $power->off : $power->off( force => 1 )` behind `$force`), the END block does not look at either `$gentle` or `IDRAC_LIVE_FORCE_OFF`.

How it triggers (only `IDRAC_LIVE_DESTRUCTIVE=1`, server found `off`): the test switches it `on`, `wait_for('on')` succeeds, then the gentle `off` or one of the `state` polls inside `wait_for( 'off', timeout => 600 )` throws (a transient `WWW::iDRAC::Error::Network`, a 409/503 from a busy controller). `$found` is still `off`, `state` now says `on`, so END sends `ForceOff` to a machine whose operating system has just booted or is in the middle of the graceful shutdown the test asked for a moment ago.

Note that the timeout path is handled correctly (`undef $found` at line 144); it is only the exception path that falls through to the forced form.

Done when: the END block uses the same decision as the switching block (gentle `off` when `power.off` is supported, `force => 1` only with `IDRAC_LIVE_FORCE_OFF=1`, otherwise only the loud `LOOK AFTER THE SERVER BY HAND` diag), and the header comment says what the restore does.

